1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,119
The most dangerous line in SQL: an UPDATE or DELETE with no WHERE clause.

2
00:00:00,119 --> 00:00:01,631
It hits every single row.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,331
Real questions need several tables.

4
00:00:09,331 --> 00:00:14,097
Each enrolment row holds a roll number and a course code: foreign keys.

5
00:00:14,097 --> 00:00:20,653
A JOIN matches each one to the primary key it points at: rolls to students, codes to courses.

6
00:00:20,867 --> 00:00:26,468
In SQL: join students to enrolment on roll, and enrolment to courses on code.

7
00:00:26,468 --> 00:00:30,323
Every enrolment row now carries a name and a course title.

8
00:00:31,534 --> 00:00:39,713
GROUP BY sorts rows into groups with the same value, here the same course, and an aggregate turns each group into one line.

9
00:00:39,713 --> 00:00:41,379
Colour the enrolments by course.

10
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:46,159
Four Computer Science rows average seventy six and a half.

11
00:00:46,159 --> 00:00:49,479
Physics, Mathematics and English follow.

12
00:00:49,479 --> 00:00:56,471
Nepali does not appear at all: with an inner join, a course with nobody enrolled has no rows to group.

13
00:00:56,471 --> 00:00:58,753
A LEFT JOIN would keep it with a count of zero.

14
00:00:58,967 --> 00:01:02,354
To filter groups rather than rows, use HAVING.

15
00:01:02,354 --> 00:01:07,588
WHERE removes rows before grouping; HAVING removes groups after.

16
00:01:07,588 --> 00:01:11,719
So students with three or more subjects is HAVING count at least three.

17
00:01:12,934 --> 00:01:15,527
And the DBMS defends its rules.

18
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:23,678
Try to enrol a student number ninety nine, who does not exist: refused, a foreign key constraint fails.

19
00:01:23,678 --> 00:01:27,844
Try to delete a course that students are enrolled in: refused again.

20
00:01:27,844 --> 00:01:29,434
That is referential integrity.

21
00:01:34,234 --> 00:01:36,176
Three database models.

22
00:01:36,176 --> 00:01:46,243
The hierarchical model is an upside down tree: every record has exactly one parent, so it handles one to many, but not many to many.

23
00:01:46,467 --> 00:01:49,698
The network model allows many parents, a graph.

24
00:01:49,698 --> 00:01:54,501
Now a student can belong to several courses and a course to several students.

25
00:01:54,734 --> 00:02:00,491
The relational model, proposed by Codd in nineteen seventy, uses no pointers at all.

26
00:02:00,491 --> 00:02:06,532
Relationships are matching values in tables, and you simply state what you want in SQL.

27
00:02:06,532 --> 00:02:09,608
Almost every database today is relational.

28
00:02:10,834 --> 00:02:15,888
Before building tables, designers draw an entity relationship diagram.

29
00:02:15,888 --> 00:02:20,132
Rectangles are entities, the things we store data about.

30
00:02:20,132 --> 00:02:23,622
Ovals are their attributes, with the key underlined.

31
00:02:23,622 --> 00:02:27,773
The diamond is a relationship: a student enrols in courses.

32
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,332
Its cardinality is many to many: a student takes many courses and a course has many students.

33
00:02:35,332 --> 00:02:42,988
In tables, a many to many relationship always becomes its own link table, here enrolment, which also holds the marks.

34
00:02:47,767 --> 00:02:51,727
Here is one table holding a school's whole result record.

35
00:02:51,727 --> 00:02:57,612
The courses column crams several values into one cell, a repeating group.

36
00:02:57,612 --> 00:03:02,118
You cannot even ask who scored above eighty in CS four oh one.

37
00:03:02,118 --> 00:03:08,284
Normalisation fixes designs like this, step by step, without losing any information.

38
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,265
First normal form: every cell holds one value.

39
00:03:13,265 --> 00:03:15,869
Give each student and course its own row.

40
00:03:15,869 --> 00:03:23,197
Now neither roll nor code alone identifies a row, but together they do: the key is roll and code.

41
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:25,225
Write down what determines what.

42
00:03:25,225 --> 00:03:26,891
Marks depend on the whole key.

43
00:03:26,891 --> 00:03:33,102
But name and faculty depend on roll alone, only part of the key: a partial dependency.

44
00:03:33,102 --> 00:03:40,133
And the faculty head depends on faculty, a non-key column: a transitive dependency.

45
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:45,664
These cause anomalies: data repeated, and things you cannot insert or delete cleanly.

46
00:03:46,867 --> 00:03:50,801
Second normal form: remove partial dependencies.

47
00:03:50,801 --> 00:04:00,745
Things that depend on roll alone move to a student table; things that depend on code alone move to a course table; what depends on the whole key stays in enrolment.

48
00:04:00,967 --> 00:04:04,875
Third normal form: remove transitive dependencies.

49
00:04:04,875 --> 00:04:12,336
The faculty head depends on faculty, not directly on the student, so it gets its own faculty table.

50
00:04:12,336 --> 00:04:14,875
Now Science's head is stored once.

51
00:04:14,875 --> 00:04:25,451
Four tables, every fact in one place, and joining them on their keys gives back every original row exactly: the decomposition is lossless.

52
00:04:26,667 --> 00:04:30,549
In summary: first normal form, atomic values.

53
00:04:30,549 --> 00:04:33,478
Second, no partial dependencies.

54
00:04:33,478 --> 00:04:36,343
Third, no transitive dependencies.

55
00:04:36,343 --> 00:04:41,070
Second normal form only matters when the key has more than one column.

56
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:45,568
And keep the foreign keys, or the tables cannot be joined back together.

57
00:04:50,367 --> 00:04:51,970
Where does the database live?

58
00:04:51,970 --> 00:04:58,884
In a centralised system, all of it sits at one site, and every branch reaches across the network.

59
00:04:58,884 --> 00:05:06,700
Easy to control and keep consistent, but every request travels, and if the server fails, everything stops.

60
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:17,331
In a distributed database, the data is split or copied across several sites, each with its own DBMS, yet it behaves as one database.

61
00:05:17,331 --> 00:05:24,752
Local questions are answered locally and the system survives one site failing, but keeping the copies in step is hard.

62
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:27,818
Finally, security.

63
00:05:27,818 --> 00:05:35,747
Authenticate users, then authorise them: GRANT and REVOKE give each user exactly the privileges they need.

64
00:05:35,747 --> 00:05:38,065
Views hide sensitive columns.

65
00:05:38,065 --> 00:05:46,644
Encrypt data, keep backups and logs, and never paste a user's input straight into a query, which is how SQL injection attacks work.

66
00:05:49,067 --> 00:05:49,837
Pause and think.

67
00:05:57,134 --> 00:05:58,373
An alternate key.

68
00:05:58,373 --> 00:06:01,735
A foreign key points to another table's primary key.

69
00:06:03,734 --> 00:06:04,169
One more.

70
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:15,239
HAVING: it filters groups after GROUP BY.

71
00:06:15,239 --> 00:06:16,838
WHERE cannot use an aggregate.

72
00:06:19,034 --> 00:06:20,116
Quick recap.

73
00:06:20,116 --> 00:06:27,486
A DBMS stores each fact once and enforces the rules, with three levels for data independence.

74
00:06:27,486 --> 00:06:29,049
The family of keys.

75
00:06:29,049 --> 00:06:33,163
DDL builds structure and DML works with rows.

76
00:06:33,163 --> 00:06:39,335
Joins match foreign keys to primary keys, and GROUP BY with HAVING summarises.

77
00:06:39,335 --> 00:06:42,838
Three models, and normalisation to third normal form.

78
00:06:47,034 --> 00:06:49,392
That was database management systems.

79
00:06:49,392 --> 00:06:54,744
Next, data communication and networking: how computers talk to each other.

80
00:06:54,744 --> 00:06:55,321
See you there.

